#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3221189b-a6e2-50f8-962a-f1b66bf2d35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314104} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc99f4-8d88-5a1b-aa8b-b022f299f233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314105} {:answer/id #uuid "d32b0d5c-2a5e-5401-b79a-3413e6f805dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314106} {:answer/id #uuid "1d85b192-c773-51bd-9307-3bf0ed37eebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314107} {:answer/id #uuid "547208ea-728c-58ba-98e1-159e175ba763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32b0d5c-2a5e-5401-b79a-3413e6f805dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314106} :answer-field/id #uuid "73602914-89c6-539e-8096-7272b5eeb3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4236c426-f250-51ed-a60b-d78d2f8899ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-122343" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acfac3bb-0043-5474-baec-f7d9aad9505b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2(t+2)^{3}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314118} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3a5543-4bab-5985-ac81-4fe61f28822a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t+8}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314119} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf1bff2-b361-5c1b-9cf1-6ff1b6485139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+1)^{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314120} {:answer/id #uuid "83791ddf-ad3a-5a14-a851-64280f33ea6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9-\\sqrt[3]{t+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314121} {:answer/id #uuid "96855702-d802-5259-a1ca-7f4b6d0a3c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96855702-d802-5259-a1ca-7f4b6d0a3c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314122} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9058f6f-069f-5ad6-86e6-f5d49d5d96c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb569efa-7827-5c95-981f-32ffff356ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122345" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=7\\,m$ and has velocity $v=12\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
